{"type":"after","callId":"call@20","endTime":50109.834,"result":{"value":{"s":"\n    Home/Manual/Developer reference/$SIR — the incoming-IR effects matrix\n    \n\n — the incoming-IR effects matrix\nWhat an IR hit does to this gun is decided by the victim's table, not by the shooter's weapon\n✅benchLast verified 2026-08-27View as Markdown\nWhat the badges mean✅bench Verified on our bench📖official Official Battle Company docs🔍app Decoded from the Callsign app👥community Community-reported🧪software Built + software-tested📐spec Specified only🚧under construction Under constructionNothing unconfirmed is published — see Credits & sourcing.\n\nThe key idea.An incoming IR word carries a 4-bit protocol (B) and a 2-bit subtype (U). The victim looks up the  row with that  key; the row's function decides what the word's 8-bit magnitude is applied to — damage, heal, armor, shield, or a status. No matching row → the hit is silently ignored. 16 × 4 = 64 addressable cells, all writable per game over BLE.Source: protocol/brx-protocol.md §5; protocol/brx-ir-protocol.md\nFormat: plays on the victim when the row fires ( \"armor suit\",  \"shields online\",  stim-pack). do not scale damage (, , , ,  all landed exactly the magnitude).Max distinct IR recognitions per game: 14 (community figure).Source: protocol/brx-protocol.md §5; docs/experiment-log.md (clean negatives)\n\nSource: protocol/brx-protocol.md §5; docs/experiment-log.md (shipped-table consequence)\nFunction map (measured at magnitude 20 on protocol 5, baseline HP 45 / armor 70 / shield 0)12 rows\n\n\nClass\nFunction ids\nMeasured behaviour\nPolarity\nConf\n\n\n\nStandard damage\n1, 4, 5, 7, 29, 30, 33, 38\n−20 per hit, drains shields → armor → HP\nenemy only\n✅bench\n\n\nArmor-piercing\n2, 6 (+17, 21 enemy-side)\nHP 45→25→5 with armor and shields untouched\nenemy only\n✅bench\n\n\n×1.25 damage\n36\nmagnitude 20 lands as 25\nenemy only\n✅bench\n\n\n×2 damage\n37\nmagnitude 20 lands as 40\nenemy only\n✅bench\n\n\nAdd HP, overflow → armor\n9, 12, 16, 19\n15→35→45, then +armor\nally only (16/19 also damage enemies)\n✅bench\n\n\nAdd HP, clamp\n10, 17\n15→35→45, no overflow\nally only (17 also AP-damages enemies)\n✅bench\n\n\nAdd HP, overflow → shield\n14, 21\n15→35→45, then +shield\nally only\n✅bench\n\n\nAdd armor\n13, 15, 20, 22\n0→20→40; overflow spills to shields\nally only (20 also strips enemy armor)\n✅bench\n\n\nAdd shield\n11, 18\n0→20→40\nally only\n✅bench\n\n\n token-2 drop\n23\nRegisters a hit, no pool change;  token 2 drops 100→0 and recovers over ~6–8 s while the gun keeps firing. The state clears on .\nenemy\n✅bench\n\n\nRegisters, no pool change\nenemy 3, 8, 24, 25, 26, 27, 28, 35 · ally 31, 32, 34\n fires, pools unchanged, no other frame.\n—\n✅bench\n\n\nNo registration\n0, 39–45 (and 28/45 on protocol 5)\n—\n—\n✅bench\n\n\nSource: docs/experiment-log.md (2026-08-26 complete two-sided $SIR map; 2026-08-27 fn 23)\nSupport functions are team-gated in firmware.With  friendlyFire = 0, heals/armor/shield grants register only from a same-team source, and damage registers only from another team. Set friendlyFire = 1 and everything lands from anyone. A medic gun enforces \"allies only\" with zero host logic.Source: protocol/brx-protocol.md §5; docs/experiment-log.md (dual-polarity, FF table)\nDEV-07diagram pendingDamage pipeline: IR word (B,U,D,C) → victim  → function multiplier → ×1.5 if crit → drain shields → armor → HP → emit  + .Source: below\napplied = magnitude × fn multiplier × (1.5 if crit)— fn 1 ×1 · fn 36 ×1.25 · fn 37 ×2; crit stacks (fn 37 + crit = ×3). ✅benchDrain order: shields → armor → HP.Armor absorbs 1:1 with no per-hit cap; overflow spills into HP (a sniper's 80 split exactly 70/10). ✅benchHeals clampat the pool max — magnitude 200 is a fill, not a stack. ✅benchNo function is a damage-over-time.18 s watched after each status hit: no ticks. ✅benchDead guns accept no IR at all.✅benchSource: protocol/brx-protocol.md §7r addendum; docs/experiment-log.md (tok5 raw magnitude, AP, heals clamp, DoT negative, 448-word brute force)\nIs there a stun?None found.  over BLE is a no-op, and fn 23 — the only function that visibly changes anything without touching a pool — leaves the gun firing.\nCan I read a native game's  table?No — the gun never reports it. Capturing an ability's IR word tells you its protocol, not what a native victim binds to it.\nWhich protocols are free?Stock uses 0, 8, 10, 11, 13 and 15 (grenade beacon); the app's table also ships rows on 1, 2, 3, 6, 9. Truly unused: 4, 5, 7, 12, 14 — but every cell is re-definable per game since you push the table.\nSource: docs/experiment-log.md (2026-08-27) · docs/experiment-log.md (2026-08-27 reframe) · protocol/brx-ir-protocol.md\n\n\n  "}},"afterSnapshot":"after@call@20"}
